<!--Post: 5007--><!--Time: 1106849020-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5007"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 10:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not only would a DMV employee have access to license records &#40;includes prior names addresses and physical descriptions&#41; but vehicle registration records as well. Years ago licenses use to indicate marital status. Because most of his kills were done on Tuesdays and Thursdays. I don&#39;t know if that would fit his schedule, unless Wichita DMV&#39;s are open on weekends. Maybe a spouse or relative could have been employed at a DMV? In California offices are open on Saturdays, but not Sundays. He would be close to retirement age now. I worked at DMV in California for many years, and yes, they have employed quite a few characters.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5007-->
<!--Post: 5014--><!--Time: 1106849748-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5014"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 10:15 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Metalhead:  an interesting side note - Most of my employees in the 70&#39;s and early 80&#39;s &#40;the examiners&#41; were former and or retired military.  Gives you something to chew on....They did at one time wear uniforms and badges, but that was stopped in the mid 70&#39;s.  I don&#39;t know about Wichita however.  Depending on your position there was a very close working contact with P.D and the courts.  We also used closely with car sales and dealerships.  Just picture it...BTK &#34;Buy The Klunker&#34; car sales.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5014-->
<!--Post: 5029--><!--Time: 1106853509-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5029"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:linnieskid@earthlink.net" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Linnieskid<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Linnieskid<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, January 27, 2005 - 11:18 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here I go again, when giving drive instructions for the road test an exmainer will always answer &#34;that is correct&#34; when an applicant asks &#34;what did you say  -turn left?&#39; or right depending on the instruction given.  You say &#34;correct&#34;  because if you told them to turn left and they asked for confirmation you could never say &#34;thats right&#34; because they would turn right.  Hard to explain but believe me &#34;that is correct&#34; is used in almost every drive test direction given. Directions must be succinct with no confusion.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
